Let the integer be $x$. We want to find the value of $x$ such that $x^2 < 2x$.

Rearranging the inequality, we have $x^2 - 2x < 0$.

Factoring out an $x$, we have $x(x-2) < 0$.

To find the values of $x$ that satisfy this inequality, we can use a sign chart:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (-3,0) -- (5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0) -- (2,0) node[right] {$x=0$};
\draw[thick] (2,0) -- (3,0) node[right] {$x=1$};
\draw[thick] (3,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x=2$};
\draw[thick] (4,0) -- (5,0) node[right] {$x=3$};
\draw[thick] (0,-1) -- (0,0) node[below] {$y=-1$};
\draw[thick] (0,0) -- (0,1) node[below] {$y=0$};
\draw[thick] (0,1) -- (0,2) node[below] {$y=1$};
\draw[thick] (0,2) -- (0,3) node[below] {$y=2$};
\draw[thick] (0,3) -- (0,3) node[below] {$y=3$};
\draw[thick] (0,0) -- (0,3) node[below] {$y=3$};
\draw[thick] (0,3) -- (0,3) node[below] {$y=3$};
\draw[thick] (0,3) -- (0,3) node[below] {$y=3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

From the sign chart, we see that the inequality $x(x-2) < 0$ holds for $0 < x < 2$.

Since we are looking for an integer solution, the only possible value for $x$ is $\boxed{1}$.
